## Files
- app/internal/analytics/interactive/components/InteractiveMode.module.css (modify)

## What to do
Three interactive elements suppress focus outlines, breaking keyboard accessibility:

1. **.progressBarWrapper** (line 100-107): Has no focus indicator. It's a button element.
2. **.exitButton** (line 147-164): Has no focus indicator. Used for close and music buttons.
3. **.cardsWrapper** (line 179-189): Has 'outline: none' which removes focus ring.

Add :focus-visible rules for each that show an accessible focus indicator without affecting mouse/touch users:

```css
.progressBarWrapper:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bumi-primary-light);
    outline-offset: 2px;
    border-radius: 4px;
}

.exitButton:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bumi-primary-light);
    outline-offset: 2px;
}

.cardsWrapper:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bumi-primary-light);
    outline-offset: -2px;
}
```

Place each :focus-visible rule immediately after the existing hover/base rules for the respective class.

## Don't
- Remove existing styles
- Change the outline:none on .cardsWrapper (it's needed for mouse) — :focus-visible overrides it for keyboard
- Add :focus rules (only :focus-visible)
- Change any colors or spacing of existing elements

## Files
- app/internal/analytics/interactive/hooks/useBackgroundMusic.ts (modify)

## What to do
The mount autoplay effect (lines 24-33) always tries to play audiosRef.current[0] (track 0) and sets currentTrackRef.current = 0, regardless of what currentCardId resolves to via targetTrack. This can race with the track-switch effect.

Fix: use the resolved targetTrack for the initial autoplay instead of hardcoded 0.

The targetTrack is computed on line 11: const targetTrack = CARD_TO_TRACK[currentCardId] ?? 0;

But the mount effect (useEffect with [] deps) runs before targetTrack is meaningful. The fix is to compute the initial target inside the mount effect or pass it as a ref.

Change the mount effect (lines 14-41) to:
```ts
useEffect(() => {
    audiosRef.current = [1, 2, 3].map((n) => {
        const audio = new Audio(`/audio/${n}.mp3`);
        audio.loop = true;
        audio.preload = 'auto';
        audio.volume = 0.5;
        return audio;
    });

    // Use the initial targetTrack (derived from currentCardId at mount)
    const initialTrack = CARD_TO_TRACK[currentCardId] ?? 0;
    audiosRef.current[initialTrack]
        ?.play()
        .then(() => {
            hasStartedRef.current = true;
            currentTrackRef.current = initialTrack;
            setIsPlaying(true);
        })
        .catch(() => {
            // Autoplay blocked - will start on user interaction
        });

    return () => {
        audiosRef.current.forEach((audio) => {
            audio.pause();
            audio.src = '';
        });
    };
// eslint-disable-next-line react-hooks/exhaustive-deps
}, []);
```

Note: We intentionally keep [] deps (mount-only) but read currentCardId's initial value. Add the eslint-disable comment to suppress the exhaustive-deps warning since we only want the mount value.

## Don't
- Change the track-switch effect (lines 44-63)
- Change the startMusic, toggleMute, or setVolume callbacks
- Change the CARD_TO_TRACK mapping
- Add new state variables

## Files
- app/internal/analytics/interactive/page.tsx (modify)
- app/internal/analytics/interactive/hooks/useCardNavigation.ts (modify)

## What to do
Two related issues with event handling:

### 1. handleCardClick captures clicks from interactive children (page.tsx lines 149-160)
When a user clicks a button, link, or input inside a card, the click bubbles up to handleCardClick and triggers unwanted navigation.

In page.tsx, modify handleCardClick to check the event target:
```ts
const handleCardClick = useCallback((e: React.MouseEvent<HTMLDivElement>) => {
    // Don't navigate when clicking interactive elements inside cards
    const target = e.target as Element;
    if (target.closest('button, a, input, textarea, select, [role="button"]')) {
        return;
    }

    const rect = e.currentTarget.getBoundingClientRect();
    const clickX = e.clientX - rect.left;
    const midpoint = rect.width / 2;

    if (clickX < midpoint) {
        goPrev();
    } else {
        goNext();
    }
}, [goNext, goPrev]);
```

### 2. Global keyboard listener captures events from interactive elements (useCardNavigation.ts lines 122-135)
The spacebar handler calls e.preventDefault() globally, interfering with buttons and inputs.

In useCardNavigation.ts, add a guard at the top of handleKeyDown:
```ts
const handleKeyDown = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
    const target = e.target as HTMLElement;
    if (target.tagName === 'INPUT' || target.tagName === 'TEXTAREA' || target.tagName === 'BUTTON' || target.isContentEditable) {
        return;
    }

    if (e.key === 'ArrowRight' || e.key === ' ') {
        e.preventDefault();
        goNext();
    } else if (e.key === 'ArrowLeft') {
        e.preventDefault();
        goPrev();
    }
};
```

## Don't
- Change the navigation logic (goNext, goPrev, goToCard)
- Remove the keyboard listener entirely
- Change the click area split (left/right midpoint)
- Add new state or hooks
